#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:5
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Geolocation Editor allows for adding and editing GPS coordinates of "
"picture metadata. The geo-data is stored into the image (EXIF tags) and the "
"location can be displayed in an internal or external browser map. The "
"Geolocation Editor also offers a <firstterm>reverse geolocation</firstterm> "
"tool."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: keyword
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:11
#, no-c-format
msgid "<keyword>GPS</keyword>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: keyword
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:12
#, no-c-format
msgid "<keyword>Map</keyword>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:16
#, no-c-format
msgid "Geolocation Editor"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:18
#, no-c-format
msgid "There are three tools regarding geolocation in &digikam;:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:24
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <link linkend=\"using-mainwindow-mapview\">Map View</link> on the Left "
"Sidebar which is the search tool for finding images by their GPS data."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:30
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Geolocation Editor we describe in this chapter and which is accessible "
"via <menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action=\"simul\">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>G</"
"keycap></keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>Item</guimenu><guimenuitem>Edit "
"Geolocation...</guimenuitem></menuchoice>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:36
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <link linkend=\"using-sidebar-maps\">Map</link> tab on the Right Sidebar "
"which shows the location of the image on a map and is purely informative."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:43
#, no-c-format
msgid "All three are based on the &marble; widget."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:47
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"These tools only work for image formats that have EXIF or XMP metadata "
"supported by <ulink url=\"http://www.exiv2.org\">Exiv2 library</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:58
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <quote>Geolocation Editor</quote> provides the tools to localize "
"pictures geographically in the common spherical coordinate system (used by "
"GPS/Galileo). Altitude, latitude and longitude, speed, number of satellites, "
"fix type and DOP are editable."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:62
#, no-c-format
msgid "Geolocation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:64
#, no-c-format
msgid "The editor has two methods to mark the pictures with coordinates:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:69
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Semi-automatic coordinate attribution to single pictures, accessible via the "
"<guilabel>Details</guilabel> tab on the Right Sidebar."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:72
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Batch correlation of GPS tracking data with a series of images you will find "
"in the <link linkend=\"geolocation-correlator\">GPS Correlator</link> tab."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:78
#, no-c-format
msgid "Furthermore the Right Sidebar offers three more tools:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:83
#, no-c-format
msgid "The <guilabel>Undo/Redo</guilabel> tab."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:86
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guilabel>Reverse Geocoding</guilabel> tab which allows to find "
"geographic names (cities, street names, ...) by using a public reverse "
"geocoding service and the GPS data of an image."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:89
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guilabel>Search</guilabel> tab which allows to find positions on the "
"map from geographic names (cities, monuments, ...) by using a public geoname "
"service (GeoNames and OpenStreetMap at the time of writing)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:94
#, no-c-format
msgid "The map"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:96
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For navigating on the map refer to &marble; handbook, Chapter 2. Also for "
"the sky context menu see &marble; documentation."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:100
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Right under the map you find a row of buttons. I shall use the tooltip texts "
"as designations in the following description. From left to right we have"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:104
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Map-settings button opens a small menu which allows you to choose "
"between Google Maps and &marble; and depending on that choice to choose the "
"kind of map you want to use. Furthermore you can select the projection for "
"&marble; (spherical, Mercator, equirectangular) and you can display a few "
"additional tools like compass and scale on the map."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:107
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Zoom-in button."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:110
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Zoom-out button."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:113
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Switch-between-markers-and-thumbnails button lets you choose whether you "
"want the positions of your images indicated by a marker symbol or by a "
"thumbnail on the map. If you choose thumbnails the next two buttons are of "
"particular interest."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:116
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Increase-the-thumbnail-size-on-the-map button is doing just that and ..."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:119
#, no-c-format
msgid "... the Decrease-the-thumbnail-size-on-the-map button as well."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:122
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Pan-mode button lets you move the map by left-click and holding on it "
"and then drag it with the mouse to where you need it. The cursor symbol will "
"change to a hand."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:125
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Zoom-into-a-group button is useful if you have images so close to each "
"other on the map that all but the topmost are obscured. Once you click on "
"the visible image the zoom factor will be adjusted so that all the other "
"images behind it become visible. If you click on a group of images and "
"nothing really happens they either have exactly the same geographic position "
"and/or the zoom factor is already at its maximum."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:128
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Select-images button toggles the selection of an image in the list of "
"images right below the buttons once you click on it on the map. The "
"selection status of all other images in the list remain unchanged."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:131
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Display-bookmarked-positions-on-the-map button is not working at the "
"time of writing because the whole bookmark issue needs to be reorganized. "
"This also affects the <menuchoice><guimenu>Bookmarks</guimenu> "
"<guimenuitem>Edit Bookmarks...</guimenuitem></menuchoice> item in the "
"context menu on the list of images below the map and the <guilabel>Add "
"Bookmark</guilabel> item in the context menu on the map."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:143
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The use of <guilabel>Add Bookmark</guilabel> in the context menu on the map "
"can lead to loss of your bookmarks in &marble;!"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:150
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"From the context menu on the map only <guilabel>Copy Coordinates</guilabel> "
"is of interest for geotagging. It copies the geographic coordinates at the "
"cursor position to the clipboard. For all other menu items see &marble; "
"handbook."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:154
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Note that you can change the layout from one map to two maps, beside each "
"other or stacked via the little drop-down menu at the bottom of the "
"Geolocation Editor."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:160
#, no-c-format
msgid "Edit Coordinates"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:161
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The coordinates can be set manually in the <guilabel>Details</guilabel> tab "
"(see sreenshot above). The location can be found and fixed iteratively with "
"the displayed map. Move mouse cursor to the region of interest, zoom in, "
"adjust location, zoom in again, and so on until sufficient accuracy is "
"achieved. Note that the zoom center will always be at the cursor position. "
"Eventually you right click at the desired position and choose <guilabel>Copy "
"Coordinates</guilabel>. Now you can go to the list of images below the map, "
"select one or more images, right-click on them and choose <guilabel>Paste "
"Coordinates</guilabel>. The coordinates will then be displayed in the "
"Latitude and Longitude fields to the right of the map. To save your changes "
"you click the <guilabel>Apply</guilabel> button."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:165
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you have one or more images that already have GPS data you can copy the "
"coordinates from one of those and paste them to one or more other images by "
"using the respective items from the context menu on the list of images. This "
"comes in handy if there is a series of photos taken at the same location."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:169
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"To the right of the map there are beside latitude and longitude fields for "
"altitude, speed, number of satellites, fix type and uncertainty (DOP). You "
"may see these fields already filled in if you select a photograph where your "
"camera wrote these information into the EXIF data. Otherwise you can fill "
"them in manually if you have these data from somewhere else, &eg; a separate "
"GPS receiver. Only for <guilabel>Altitude</guilabel> the context menu on the "
"list of images offers <guilabel>Look up missing altitude values</guilabel> "
"which causes the editor to look up these data in the map data provided the "
"position (latitude and longitude) is already assigned to the photograph."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:173
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Other than that the context menu on the list of images offer items to remove "
"some of the data from the image. Regarding the last item "
"<guilabel>Bookmarks</guilabel> see <link linkend=\"bookmarkstatus.anchor"
"\">here</link>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:180
#, no-c-format
msgid "The correlator"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:181
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In order to correlate your images with geographic data you need to have a "
"GPS tracking information available as a XML file in <literal>gpx</literal> "
"format (<application><ulink url=\"http://www.gpsbabel.org\">gpsbabel</"
"ulink></application> and <application><ulink url=\"http://www.ncc.up.pt/"
"gpsman/\">gpsman</ulink></application> can download and convert tracking "
"data from a GPS device for you)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:189
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<menuchoice><guimenu>Image</guimenu> <guimenuitem>Geolocation</guimenuitem> "
"<guimenuitem>Correlator</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. This interface can "
"correlate a previously selected number of pictures with registered GPS "
"tracking information."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:198
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The geo-correlation dialog based on EXIF date/time and tracking data (gpx "
"format)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:202
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Select the images you want to correlate in the application main view, then "
"call the menu. The above dialog will show up. To indicate possible time/"
"location correlation you have to load a track file with <guilabel>Load GPX "
"File</guilabel> that contains GPS data taken at the same time and location "
"as the pictures with your camera."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:206
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When the file is loaded, an automatic correlation is done showing the "
"attributed coordinates in red. GPS track data is invariably recorded in GMT "
"time, so you need to match the camera time with GMT, which can be done with "
"<guilabel>Time Zone</guilabel>. Select the <emphasis>camera time zone</"
"emphasis> here!"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:212
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guilabel>Max. time gap</guilabel> setting specifies the limit within "
"which GPS time and camera time shall be deemed coincident. The maximum value "
"is 2000 seconds."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:217
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you have no tracking data for pictures that were taken in the same place "
"you can just manually edit a <literal>gpx</literal> file and apply it to "
"those images. The 2000 second time gap will then correlate all pictures "
"taken within 20 minutes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:223
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The option <guilabel>Interpolate</guilabel> and <guilabel>Difference in min."
"</guilabel> can be used if there is no match of data. If you know that in "
"reality there is a location match despite a larger time gap between camera "
"and GPS, you can set this limit here and click on the <guilabel>Correlate</"
"guilabel> button to interpolate."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:231
#, no-c-format
msgid "Correlation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:234
#, no-c-format
msgid "240 minutes is the maximum time difference that can be introduced here."
msgstr ""

